  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 5.3. Increased capacity of the ILO constituents to design and deliver innovative, flexible and inclusive learning options, encompassing work-based learning and quality apprenticeships</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">In collaboration with the Somali Chamber of Commerce &amp; Industries (SCCI), Somalia&apos;s private sector implemented during the course of 2020 flexible and inclusive skills development programmes for the renewable energy sector focusing on productivity and sustainability within enterprises. Two private sector companies, namely Banadir Technical Institute (BTI) in Mogadishu and National Energy Corporation of Somalia (NECSOM) in Garowe adopted new and innovative curricula on basic electrical services and solar PV in 2020. To deliver these new training programmes effectively, including through workbased learning, the private sector enhanced their capacities to train junior technicians on electrical and mechanical and plumbing skills.

BTI hired 53 of the 100 trained technicians in May 2021. BECO, the largest electricity supplier in the country, is planning to employ the remaining students. The ILO supported the training of 18 trainers (16 men and 2 women) nominated by the Somalia Independent Power Producer company who were trained on basic electrical and solar PV skills in Nairobi, Kenya. The ILO also supported the development and translation of the training materials.

With support from the Swedish cooperation, the ILO responded to the challenges faced by COVID-19 by putting in place business continuity plans and adopting digital tools to ensure the targeted private sector partners set up online platforms to deliver the trainings.</narrative>
   </description>
